  {
    "episode_id": 80574,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/0/80574.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "So it's good to be with you. I'm originally from Brooklyn, New York. Born and raised, Crown Heights, Brooklyn. My mom was single mother. My older sister is 10 years older than me. My younger sister is 10 years younger than me. My younger sister. And so, you know, it was tough. We were struggling, grew up poor. My mom put me into sports. She didn't want me hanging out on the street, so she made sure I was always in sports or always at museums, things like that. Always wanted me to be focused on education. Her advice, and I'd still hear it to this day in my mind, is don't follow the crowd. Think things through logically. Research everything for yourself. Just don't listen to people. And so when I was in my career, this is, you know, fast forward, I'm in my late 20s and it's around the financial collapse. I had already started my career in banking and was now working in insurance. And when I was looking at the financial collapse and trying to do research to help our clients and help our investors, I started to realize that I had major disagreements with the political policies of the Democrat Party, even though I wasn't registered Democrat. from there I started reading about political philosophy and some modern politics but mostly philosophy and realize that was quite conservative.",
